BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Research Talks featuring Dr. Sugandha Gaur
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T180000
UID:research-talks-featuring-dr-sugandha-gaur@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the November edition of Research Talks\, featuring
  Dr. Sugandha Gaur. In this talk - “Advertising Futures: a community-int
 erest industry" Dr. Gaur asks What if the way we approach advertising cons
 idered capital as a tool for social transformation?\n\nJoin us for the Nov
 ember edition of Research Talks featuring Dr. Sugandha Gaur presenting Adv
 ertising Futures: a community-interest industry What if the way we approac
 h advertising considered capital as a tool for social transformation? Brie
 f Description of the talk: This talk explores the work of the Advertising 
 Action Collective \, a research-led initiative that prepares creatives as 
 advertising actionists reimagining a socially responsive industry. Further
 \, it proposes stakeholders\, i.e.\, academia\, brands\, industry\, and co
 nsumers\, an opportunity to investigate and implement alternative framewor
 ks for collective action by positioning capital not only as an economic re
 source but also as a tool for positive social impact. Through a series of 
 "What if?" questions\, this session will encourage attendees to reassess t
 raditional advertising approaches and imagine community-interest creative 
 futures. Speaker Bio: Sugandha Gaur\, Ph.D. (they/them) Associate Professo
 r | Advertising Actionist\, Faculty of Design Dr. Sugandha Gaur is an Adve
 rtising Actionist whose work spans education\, research\, and artistic pra
 ctice. With over fifteen years of experience\, they focus on transforming 
 ideas into collective action. Holding a Ph.D. in Media Arts\, Sugandha bri
 ngs a strong foundation in creative and unconventional communication strat
 egies to address complex social and environmental challenges. Their work i
 s grounded in a commitment to land-based pedagogies and the recognition of
  Indigenous knowledges\, guided by a belief in creativity as a catalyst fo
 r care and collective transformation. They are currently an Associate Prof
 essor in the Advertising Program at OCAD University\, where they teach acr
 oss all four years and lead the Advertising Action Collective— a researc
 h-led initiative that prepares creatives as advertising actionists\, reima
 gining a socially responsive industry. It is a space that proposes an oppo
 rtunity for stakeholders\, i.e.\, academia\, brands\, industry\, to invest
 igate and implement alternative frameworks for collective action by positi
 oning capital not only as an economic resource but also as a tool for posi
 tive social impact. Through this space\, their goal is for all the stakeho
 lders to reassess traditional advertising approaches and imagine community
 -interest creative futures. Microsoft Teams meeting Join: https://teams.mi
 crosoft.com/meet/221631360770329?p=eDP3o8Z8DsCrqtpMkW Meeting ID: 221 631 
 360 770 329 Passcode: Sz27kb9r This event is part of the Research Talks se
 ries organized by the Office of Research and Innovation\, and is free to a
 ttend for everyone. For more information on the event or if you would like
  to present your work in this series\, feel free to reach out to tavleenla
 ll@ocadu.ca or research@ocadu.ca Date and Time: Wednesday\, November 4\, 4
  - 6 p.m. Location: Room 322 - 230 Richmond St W. Office of Research & Inn
 ovation OCAD University Instagram: ocadu_research email: research@ocadu.ca
LOCATION:230 Richmond (RHB) 230 Richmond St. W Room 322
URL:https://teams.microsoft.com/meet/221631360770329?p=eDP3o8Z8DsCrqtpMkW
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sabbatical Presentations - Michelle Forsyth\, Anda Kubis\, and Der
 ek Sullivan
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T103003
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261104T120000
UID:sabbatical-presentations-michelle-forsyth-anda-kubis-and-derek-sulliva
 n@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:A Hybrid Event: Sabbatical Presentations by Three Faculty Memb
 ers Michelle Forsyth | Cross-Disciplinary / Drawing and Painting Anda Kubi
 s | Drawing and Painting Derek Sullican | Sculpture/Installation\n\nFacult
 y of Art Sabbatical Presentations Wednesday\, November 4\, 2026\, 10:30 am
  to 12:00 pm 230 Richmond St.\, W\, Room 322 Hybrid Presentation Join Team
 s\n\nMichelle Forsyth Lil' Pinky \, 2026 archival inkjet print\, 36 x 54 i
 nches\n\nMichelle Forsyth presents: When I Close My Eyes\, I See Orange I 
 am interested in the way disability reorganizes my material world. Objects
  that might otherwise seem neutral begin to take on new meaning when they 
 are relied upon repeatedly\, repositioned for access\, modified\, or broug
 ht close enough to my body to grab. I have been taking photographs of thes
 e things\; I want to slow down the act of looking and ask what such object
 s can reveal about the lived textures of disability. The seemingly ordinar
 y things that are often overlooked. In addition to these photographs\, I a
 lso completed a manuscript for a memoir entitled: When I Close My Eyes I S
 ee Orange. I am still searching for a publisher.\n\nAnda Kubis presents: T
 he Path Rooted in the rhythms of rural Ontario\, where my studio is situat
 ed\, my practice has grown through walking\, close observation\, seasonal 
 change\, and material experimentation. Through these daily rituals\, I see
 k a deeper connection to place and a more vivid sense of presence. Patienc
 e lies at the heart of my work. Beneath the quietude of rural life\, anxie
 ties still emerge. Perceived threats from new technologies\, the encroachm
 ent of phones\, and the relentless news cycle remind me that presence must
  be continually sought and recovered through my studio practice. A prolifi
 c year immersed in conventional and digital painting\, watercolour\, eco-d
 yeing\, and explorations connected to cycles of time and natural elements 
 became a dialogue between intention and spontaneity\, discovery and uncert
 ainty—a way of learning through materials and becoming more attuned to t
 he world directly around me. The Path is a practice of attention\, care\, 
 and discovery – a way of sustaining health.\n\nDerek Sullivan presents: 
 Several Trailheads: January to June 2026 My sabbatical research began with
  a specific place: the rural studio that my partner and I built together i
 n 2020–21 near Tamworth\, Ontario. From there\, my walking took me furth
 er afield\, including time spent in the University of Toronto libraries\, 
 work with historic type and printing presses through a Massey College Fell
 owship\, and travel to London and Yorkshire in the UK. Neil and I had spon
 taneously begun organizing stones dislodged during construction into a dry
 -stone wall. Our wall became an orienting device for exploring the history
 /technique of this form and its connections to colonization in rural Ontar
 io and beyond. Through this research\, I initiated new works in drawing an
 d sculpture\, completed a new relief print edition\, and realized a two-pe
 rson exhibition with Ella Gonzales\, and has opened several routes to cont
 inue exploring.
LOCATION:230 Richmond (RHB) 230 Richmond St. W RHB 322
URL:https://teams.microsoft.com/meet/233627362444288?p=VjiLNlKKZh7vRbIU9k
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
